+# ADR 0005: Windowed chat rows with one scroll owner
+
+## Status
+
+Accepted for the local HAPI DSH extension; upstream proposal drafted.
+
+## Context
+
+One 200-message HAPI page can project to about 700 chat rows. Rendering every row on each prepend produced animation-frame gaps above 600 ms in the mobile history smoke, even when the source page committed successfully. HAPI's browser reader avoids this cost by mounting the visible range and a small overscan while its viewport remains the only owner of scroll position and anchor correction.
+
+## Decision
+
+Virtualize rows at the official Chat flow boundary with stable semantic row keys, measured dynamic heights, and spacers for unmounted ranges. Keep the current reading row, navigation destination, focused row, and retained expanded groups mounted. Leave scroll restoration, follow behavior, and prepend correction with the existing Chat viewport. Keep upward gesture detection and source-page warming in the HAPI DSH host integration, outside the official Chat component. Warmed pages remain private until the source cursor confirms a complete page; foreground failure preserves the current transcript and can be retried.
+
+Keep the upstream 200-message page policy. Smaller pages do not solve the rendering cost and would increase request frequency on long transcripts.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The client mounts only visible and retained rows, so prepending a large transcript does not reconcile every loaded row. Semantic anchors survive index changes as older rows arrive. Page transport and page rendering remain separate: a warm-up cannot change the visible window, and a completed page is committed once. The extension still needs real browser and physical-device acceptance for scroll feel; component tests do not establish that claim.
+
+## Verification
+
+The patch includes anchor-preservation, bounded-mounted-row, focused-row retention, and dynamic-measurement tests. HAPI DSH separately tests private page warm-up, single-flight cursor reuse, failure recovery, and reader-intent admission.
