+# ADR 0006: Process groups share the transcript scroll owner
+
+## Status
+
+Accepted for the local HAPI DSH extension; upstream proposal drafted.
+
+## Context
+
+HAPI DSH's process-group body was capped at 400px and used `overflow-y: auto`. A wheel or touch gesture beginning in that body scrolled the group instead of the transcript, so the history reader could not observe the gesture or request the next source page. The group also maintained a separate scroll-follow controller. This differs from HAPI's history reader, where message content belongs to one transcript scrollport.
+
+## Decision
+
+Treat the process-group title as a disclosure. A closed group shows its bounded preview; opening it removes the height cap and places all members in the normal transcript flow. Do not create a scrollport or scroll-follow controller inside a process group. Keep the transcript viewport as the sole owner of scrolling and history paging.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Wheel and touch input over a process group continue to move the full conversation and reach the same history paging path as other rows. Expanded content is fully visible and participates in normal transcript measurement and anchoring. The real-browser history smoke rejects any process-group body that computes to `overflow-y: auto` or `scroll`.
+
+## Verification
+
+The client component test checks that opening a process group changes it from preview to expanded flow without publishing inner-scroll state. The release smoke checks the actual production DOM and repeats desktop wheel and mobile touch paging, anchor retention, round-trip reading, and frame-gap acceptance.
